a poll that produces no message still retains: True
the retention ignores frame length, so the survivor is not a copy of the frame: True
building the frame costs more than the records retain: True
and the frame cost DOES grow with frame length: True
the three stages of the path account for the whole, with no residual: True
ONE transport record per frame: feeding builds exactly one: True
both feeds build one record, so the saving was the CALLER's construction: True
a frame the stack RECOGNISES costs more than one it drops: True
and the reading still grows with frame length on this fixture, which is NOT a copy in the stack because the IPv4 branch no longer makes one, and is unattributed: True
eth-payload, which the IPv4 branch no longer calls, would alone have cost more than a whole refused frame now does: True
reading each element with list-at costs the same as pushing a constant, so list-at is not the difference: True
and eth-payload still costs MORE than that same loop, so its cost is NOT in the loop: True
the SAME eth-payload over the same bytes costs more when the frame was built by concatenation: True
the zeros fixture's IP stage costs LESS than one payload copy, so it cannot have copied the payload and its reading is a refusal: True
an ACCEPTED frame's IP stage still costs MORE than a refused one, which is the reach control now that no copy is left to make it dear: the pseudo-header is built only past ip-header-valid: True
the IPv4 branch no longer copies the frame, so an accepted frame and the IP stage over the same bytes cost the SAME: True
and the two copies that were removed exceed the whole of what an accepted frame now costs: True
and a whole accepted frame now costs LESS than ONE copy of its payload, which is what the campaign was for: True
that figure is the PARSED-AND-VALID path and NOT the delivery path: this fixture's connection is closed, so tcp-step answers ActNone and tcp-seg-payload is never called: True
and tcp-seg-payload, which NOTHING on the receive path calls any more, is what that path would have cost: it still scales with the payload: True
what remains of an accepted frame is the parse, the checksum's pseudo-header and one NetResult, with no residual: True
and the parse itself no longer scales with the payload, because the segment carries a RANGE rather than a list: True
the two IP lists that harness builds per iteration are exactly the ip-src and ip-dst the stage builds, so the identity holds with the raw term: True
tcp-checksum-valid no longer scales with the segment, because it carries the pseudo-header's sum instead of concatenating: True
and the state machine below it does not: net-receive-segment is smaller than any one of the copies: True
a concatenated copy is CHEAPER to build than a pushed copy of the same bytes, because & allocates the exact sum and list-push doubles: True
the range feed and the list feed put the SAME bytes in the receive buffer: True
and the range feed honours its offset, which a whole-buffer comparison at 0 could not tell: True
the ramp is not all zeros, so those comparisons could have failed: True
and the comparator itself refuses two feeds that differ, so the two arms above are not an instrument that cannot fail: True
and the range feed allocates no more than the list feed does once the list exists, which is one record each: True
on the SEND path, tcp-with-checksum no longer scales with the segment, because it sums the pseudo-header instead of concatenating it: True
and it costs the same on an ODD-length segment, which is the case the pad it dropped existed for: True
a segment it signs is accepted by the receiver, at even and at odd lengths: True
and an UNSIGNED segment is refused, so that round-trip is not an instrument that cannot fail: True
